# PromQL quick reference
[group('doc'), no-exit-message]
cheatsheet:
    #!/usr/bin/env -S colored-md

    # PROMQL QUICK REFERENCE

    ## SELECTORS

    ```
    metric{label="v"}       instant vector - exact-match filter
        # every `up` series where job label is exactly "haproxy" (most recent values in lookback window)
        up{job="haproxy"}

    metric{label!="v"}      not-equal filter
        # every `up` series EXCEPT job=prometheus
        up{job!="prometheus"}

    metric{label=~"re"}     regex match (RE2 syntax)
        # `up` for any instance ending in :8080
        up{instance=~".*:8080"}

    metric{label!~"re"}     regex not-match
        # `up` for jobs whose name does NOT start with "probe"
        up{job!~"probe.*"}

    metric[5m]              range vector - ALL samples in the last 5 minutes
        # not useful on its own; feeds functions like rate(), irate(), increase()
        http_requests_total[5m]

    metric[5m] offset 1h    same window, but shifted 1h back in time
        # 5m rate, but from 1 week ago - handy for week-over-week comparisons
        rate(http_requests_total[5m] offset 1w)

    metric @ 1609459200     evaluate at an absolute unix timestamp
        # value of `up` at 2021-01-01 00:00 UTC (independent of "now")
        up @ 1609459200

    NOTE: regex label matchers (`=~`, `!~`) are FULLY ANCHORED. `=~"api"` is `=~"^api$"`.
          Use `.*` on both ends for substring (`=~".*api.*"`), or `=~"api.*"` for prefix.
    ```

    ## STALENESS / LOOKBACK

    ```
    lookback_delta (5m)     instant query at T returns last sample within 5m of T (NOT "this second")
        # `up` stays "1" for ~5m AFTER a target stops responding
        # scrape_interval (15s, 60s, ...) is separate - lookback governs staleness, not scrape cadence
        up

    series drop-out         a series disappears from results only after > lookback_delta with no fresh sample
        # `up == 0` alerts fire slightly DELAYED after real outage (Prometheus waits out the last "1")
        up{job="dead-target"} == 0

    tuning                  server flag `--query.lookback-delta=<duration>` on Prometheus/Mimir
        # no per-query override - lookback is server-level, not a PromQL knob
    ```

    ## COUNTERS (rate family - always wrap counters!)

    ```
    rate(c[5m])             per-second rate, averaged across a 5m window
        # requests-per-second, smoothed - typical alerting/graphing input
        rate(http_requests_total[5m])

    irate(c[5m])            per-second rate from the LAST 2 samples in the window
        # spiky/instant rate - live dashboards, NOT alerts (too jittery)
        irate(http_requests_total[1m])

    increase(c[5m])         total counter increase over the window
        # how many requests happened in the last 5 minutes (rate * window)
        increase(http_requests_total[5m])

    resets(c[5m])           number of times the counter reset in the window
        # how many times did the process restart in 24h
        resets(process_uptime_seconds[24h])
    ```

    ## AGGREGATION (over instant vector)

    ```
    sum by (label) (v)      group by label; drop every other label
        # total request-rate per job (all instances/status/etc collapsed)
        sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))

    sum without (label) (v) group by every label EXCEPT the one listed
        # request-rate summed across instances, keeping job/status/etc
        sum without (instance) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))

    topk(N, v) / bottomk(N, v)   top or bottom N series
        # the 3 busiest jobs by request rate
        topk(3, sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m])))

    quantile(0.95, v)       p95 ACROSS series (not over time - rarely what you want)
        # for latency-over-time use histogram_quantile() below
        quantile(0.95, http_request_duration_seconds)

    sum, avg, min, max, count, stddev, stdvar        # same shape as `sum by`
    ```

    ## COMPARISON

    ```
    v > 0                   filter - only keep samples matching the condition
        # only currently-down series (returns just the down ones)
        up == 0

    v > bool 0              return 1 (true) or 0 (false) instead of filtering
        # 1 when error rate exceeds 0.1/s, 0 otherwise - handy for alert expressions
        (rate(errors[5m]) > bool 0.1)

    v == 1                  equality (also !=, <=, >=)
        # only the currently-up series
        up == 1
    ```

    ## TIME

    ```
    time()                  current unix time in seconds
        # process uptime in seconds
        time() - process_start_time_seconds

    timestamp(v)            timestamp of each sample (unix seconds)
        # seconds since last scrape - detects stale/lagging targets
        time() - timestamp(up)

    hour(v), day_of_week(v), month(v), year(v)   calendar helpers (UTC)
        # true (=1) only during UTC morning hours
        hour(vector(time())) < 12
    ```

    ## HISTOGRAMS

    ```
    histogram_quantile(q, sum by (le) (rate(<metric>_bucket[range])))
        # p95 request duration over the last 5 minutes
        # KEY: always sum by (le) FIRST, then quantile - reversing gives garbage
        histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))
    ```

    ## LABEL MANIPULATION

    ```
    label_replace(v, "dst", "$1", "src", "re")   extract via regex capture
        # copy the host part of instance="host:port" into a new "host" label
        label_replace(up, "host", "$1", "instance", "(.+):.*")

    label_join(v, "dst", "-", "a", "b")          concat labels with separator
        # create label host_job = "<job>-<instance>"
        label_join(up, "host_job", "-", "job", "instance")
    ```

    ## COMMON PATTERNS

    ```
    # error rate: what fraction of requests returned 5xx over the last 5m?
    sum(rate(reqs{code=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(reqs[5m]))

    # p95 latency: what was the 95th-percentile request duration in the last 5m?
    histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))

    # availability: what percentage of the last hour was `up`?
    avg_over_time(up[1h]) * 100

    # existence probe: did this metric emit ANY sample in the last 24h?
    count_over_time(metric[24h]) > 0

    # counter delta: how many events happened in the last hour?
    increase(http_requests_total[1h])

    # week-over-week comparison: how much did request rate change vs a week ago?
    rate(reqs[5m]) - rate(reqs[5m] offset 1w)
    ```

    ## SEE ALSO

    - `mimir doc` for the full list of PromQL / Prometheus / Mimir doc links.

# Graphite -> Mimir HAProxy metric map
[group('doc')]
graphite-haproxy:
    #!/usr/bin/env -S colored-md

    # HAProxy: Graphite -> Mimir metric name map

    Prometheus exporter metric names are prefixed with `haproxy_backend_` or
    `haproxy_frontend_` depending on the stats layer. HTTP response code
    split (`hrsp_1xx`, `hrsp_2xx`, ...) collapses into a single metric with
    a `code` label rather than a name suffix.

    | Graphite | Mimir metric                     |
    | -------- | -------------------------------- |
    | act      | active_servers                   |
    | bin      | bytes_in_total                   |
    | bout     | bytes_out_total                  |
    | chkfail  | check_failures_total             |
    | chkdown  | check_up_down_total              |
    | cli_abrt | client_aborts_total              |
    | ctime    | connect_time_average_seconds     |
    | dreq     | requests_denied_total            |
    | dresp    | responses_denied_total           |
    | eresp    | response_errors_total            |
    | econ     | connection_errors_total          |
    | hrsp     | http_responses_total             |
    | lbtot    | loadbalanced_total               |
    | qcur     | current_queue                    |
    | qmax     | max_queue                        |
    | rate     | current_session_rate             |
    | rate_max | max_session_rate                 |
    | req_tot  | http_requests_total              |
    | rtime    | response_time_average_seconds    |
    | scur     | current_sessions                 |
    | slim     | limit_sessions                   |
    | smax     | max_sessions                     |
    | stot     | sessions_total                   |
    | srv_abrt | server_aborts_total              |
    | status   | status                           |
    | weight   | weight                           |
    | wredis   | redispatch_warnings_total        |
    | wretr    | retry_warnings_total             |

    # dlb tenant: Graphite path -> Mimir label

    For the classic `loadbalancers.group.<group>.<host>.haproxy.role.<role>.backend.<backend>.<metric>` layout:

    | Graphite path node | Mimir label            |
    | ------------------ | ---------------------- |
    | group              | $DOMAIN_persona        |
    | host (node 3)      | host_name              |
    | role               | role                   |
    | backend            | proxy                  |

    Note: `group` in Graphite carbon was derived from hostname; in Mimir the
    closest equivalent is `$DOMAIN_persona` (e.g. `corporatelb`, `internallb`).

